You are the founding engineer responsible for building Verik from an empty repository.

Verik is an independent verification runtime for AI-generated code.

The central thesis is:

The system writing code should not be the only system deciding whether that code is safe to ship.

Verik allows a developer to execute any coding agent or development command through a local CLI:

    verik run -- claude
    verik run -- codex
    verik run -- aider
    verik run -- <any command and arguments>

Verik records the repository before the command runs, executes the command transparently, captures the resulting repository changes, and independently verifies those changes through four isolated stages:

    Scout
    Builder
    Reviewer
    Judge

The initial product is a local-first CLI and verification engine.

Do NOT build a VS Code extension, web dashboard, GitHub App, billing system, or Claude-specific integration in this milestone.

The CLI and verification engine must be designed so those integrations can call the same internal APIs later.

==================================================
1. PRODUCT BEHAVIOR
==================================================

The primary command is:

    verik run -- <command> [arguments...]

Example:

    verik run -- claude -p "Add password reset support"

Expected lifecycle:

1. Validate that the current directory is a supported Git repository.
2. Inspect the current repository state.
3. Capture a baseline snapshot before executing the child command.
4. Start a Verik run.
5. Execute the supplied command in an interactive subprocess.
6. Preserve stdin, stdout, stderr, terminal colors, signals, and exit code behavior.
7. Wait for the child command to finish.
8. Capture the final repository state.
9. Determine exactly what changed during the command.
10. Build a structured verification context.
11. Execute Scout.
12. Execute Builder.
13. Execute Reviewer.
14. Execute Judge.
15. Print a concise terminal verdict.
16. Store the full evidence report locally.
17. Exit using a documented Verik exit code.

Verik must not require the wrapped command to know that Verik exists.

This must work:

    verik run -- bash
    verik run -- npm run generate
    verik run -- python script.py
    verik run -- claude
    verik run -- codex

The double dash marks the end of Verik options and the beginning of the wrapped command.

Additional commands:

    verik verify
    verik report
    verik explain
    verik status
    verik init
    verik config
    verik version

Behavior:

    verik verify

Verifies the current uncommitted Git diff without executing another command.

    verik report

Prints the most recent complete report.

    verik report <run-id>

Prints a specific historical report.

    verik explain

Explains the latest verdict in plain English.

    verik status

Shows whether the current repository is configured, the latest run, provider status, and repository state.

    verik init

Creates a `.verik` directory and a readable configuration file.

